CHAPTER IV
FORMS OF VIOLENCE
Article 5
Forms of violence against women and children include: a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. neglect;
e. coercion/deprivation of liberty;
f. exploitation;
g. threat of certain actions; and/or
h. other violence.

CHAPTER II
SCOPE
Article 2
The scope of protection of women and children from acts of violence is as follows:
a. principles and objectives;
b. forms of violence;
c. rights of women and children victims of violence;
d. obligations and responsibilities;
e. prevention of acts of violence;
f. special protection of children;
g. services and empowerment of victims of acts of violence;
h. institutions;
i. cooperation and partnership;
j. reporting;
k. guidance;
k. guidance and supervision; and
l. financing.

Article 9
(1) The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 8 letter a, include:
  a. implementing policies for the protection of women and children from acts of violence established by the government;
  b. establishing policies, programs, and activities for the protection of women and children from acts of violence;
  c. conducting cooperation in the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
  d. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
  e. allocating a budget for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence in accordance with regional financial capacity; and
  f. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence.
(2) In order to carry out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regent establishes action programs and activities for the protection of women and children in one Regional Action Plan as a basis for OPD and

CHAPTER III
PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES
Article 3
Protection of women and children from acts of violence and discrimination is organized based on the principles of:
a. respect for human rights;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination;
d. victim protection;
e. empowerment; and
f. integration.

CHAPTER XIII
GUIDANCE AND SUPERVISION
Article 31
(1) Regional Government is obligated to conduct guidance and supervision of the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence.
(2) Guidance as referred to in paragraph (1), includes: a. guidelines and fulfillment standards;
b. technical guidance and training;
c. provision of facilities;
d. monitoring; and
e. evaluation.
(3) Guidance as referred to in paragraph (2), in the framework of realizing the objectives of protection of the rights of women and children from acts of violence in accordance with minimum service standards implemented by OPD/related agencies and the community.

CHAPTER VII
PREVENTION OF ACTS OF VIOLENCE
Article 13
(1) To prevent the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children, the Regional Government shall conduct empowerment and awareness-raising to families, parents and the community by providing information, guidance, and/or counseling.
(2) In addition to empowerment and awareness-raising as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government shall undertake efforts as follows:
a. increasing the number and quality of educational units both formal and non-formal;
b. opening accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement, and social services;
c. opening employment opportunities for women;
d. building community participation and concern towards the prevention and protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
f. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities, and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care for women and children; and
g. opening complaint posts for the protection of women and children from acts of violence.

Article 17
(1) The forms of services provided to women and children victims of acts of violence are as follows:
a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. legal assistance;
d. rehabilitation and social reintegration;
e. repatriation;
f. identification services; and
g. psychological services.
(2) Services as referred to in paragraph (1) must be supported by facilities in the form of:
a. special service rooms within the police force;
b. expert and professional personnel;
c. service centers and safe houses; and/or
d. other facilities and infrastructure necessary for victim recovery.
(3) The forms of services as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in accordance with minimum service standards established by the government and implemented by related OPD whose duties and functions are in the fields of:
a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. women's empowerment and child protection; and
d. mental and spiritual.
(4) In carrying out the duties and functions as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government cooperates with government agencies, other regional governments, non-governmental organizations, and social institutions.
(5) Further provisions regarding the procedures for handling services for women and children from acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), paragraph (2) and paragraph (3), shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 23
In addition to establishing the PPT in the Protection of Women and Children as referred to in Article 22, in order to support the implementation of protection for women and children from acts of violence, the following shall be established:
a. Task force on criminal acts of human trafficking;
b. Regional action committee for the elimination of the worst forms of child labor;
c. Sub-district level task force for the protection of women and children;
d. Domestic violence prevention groups at the village/sub-village level; and
e. Other forms of task forces that support the Integrated Service Center for the Protection of Women and Children.

CHAPTER V
